# Editors
#
# Ignoring the contents and un-ignoring the four shared files, which is what
# github/gitignore does and what a blanket `.vscode/` gets wrong. Those files
# are project configuration meant to be committed -- extensions.json is how a
# repo tells a new contributor which extensions it expects -- and a global
# ignore of the whole directory makes `git add .vscode/extensions.json` do
# nothing at all, with no error to explain why.
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/tasks.json
!.vscode/launch.json
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/*.code-snippets

# JetBrains keeps per-user state inside a directory that also holds shared
# project settings, so the same rule applies.
.idea/**/workspace.xml
.idea/**/tasks.xml
.idea/**/usage.statistics.xml
.idea/**/dictionaries
.idea/**/shelf

*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Environments and secrets (safety net, not a replacement for the project's .gitignore)
.env
.env.local
.venv/

